ng these  harsh sanctions against russia  to the extent that it's uh creating a  havoc for european economists however by  attempting to also blame and shift blame  towards the chinese i think the us also  would like to use this crisis as a way  of  of having the europeans also decouple a  little bit from from the from the  chinese again this is  been one of the key problems of the  united states they have the europeans i  mean have always had security dependence  on the us but they've had a very hard  time to convert the security dependence  into economic loyalty but now with this  heightened security threats we see the  u.s obviously has more uh  more influence over the europeans so  they will definitely exploit this crisis  to to the extent that  they can get europeans from not only cut  off from the russians but obviously also  the chinese but not completely obviously  because that's  not possible sure and and these are so  so we say that this is the end of  globalization that the world will  will now divide into separate  potentially hostile camps regardless  of what happens in ukraine  um  yes and no because i would  like to call our attention to the facts  that  many of the countries that  either didn't vote or abstain in the  general assembly or the resolution right  than in the invasion  and they they represent a significant  proportion of the world's population  right  well significant part of the world  population but it was also a significant  number of countries from the african  continent and from asia and those  countries that somehow coincidentally  were part of  1956 and uh and bandung so these  countries are saying look these are two  big dogs fighting and what is in it for  us  in this fight so this is not our fight  we have other issues we have other  demands and so this is this is going to  redefine uh word politics and these  countries will have somehow to choose  sides uh even during even during the  cold war and were at the height of the  third world uh  the non-aligned movement uh  many of those countries were siding  slightly with one spell or the other but  they were they had another argument they  were saying we our our  fight is another one and our issues are  different and i think that these same  countries are saying the same thing when  they are  thinking the same thing where they're  looking at this uh fight between these  two big dogs under us leadership and  currently under russian leadership but  it's uh russia china um  two challenging powers challenging the  status quo power of the united states so  this is what most of the world is seeing  i think and these are is it necessarily  a bad thing that the world order is  reshaped  not a bad thing but there were good  things in the in the post um  cold war order the  the the the norms and the values imposed  by the west with  the advance of democracy and  human rights and the protection of  minorities all of these things are very  important very valuable  and people from the social forum in  porto alegre that were protesting  against davos they were they wanted  globalization but the globalization of  these norms and these values for  protecting minorities and human rights  and democracy the advance of democracy  and its values and freedom of speech and  freedom of faith so those were very  positive values that a lot of people  will miss if they have to choose camps  and they have to choose sides and there  is a side that is  more authoritarian and more uh unwilling  to be  uh observing human rights and  the rights of minorities so that is a  negative point in this uh  change that will happen inevitably very  soon  glenn is it possible that some good will  come out of this that there's an  opportunity in fact here to to create a  a more united more  interconnected and perhaps more  sustainable  uh trading world i think so because well  we have to see that this is kind of  transition from the unipolar to a  multi-polar world so after the  aft af